WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.465
This is a dam made of dirt, holding
back a lake twenty metres deep.

00:00:08.815 --> 00:00:10.673
Water soaks through dirt.

00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:14.181
So why is the back half of this dam dry?

00:00:14.531 --> 00:00:18.451
These clear tubes show how high
the water stands inside it:

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:21.265
high on the lake side, and
nothing at all at the back.

00:00:21.615 --> 00:00:26.212
How can a heap of soil hold back a
lake, when water passes through soil?

00:00:28.562 --> 00:00:30.884
Start on a beach, at low tide.

00:00:31.234 --> 00:00:34.694
For hours after the sea has
pulled back, little streams

00:00:34.694 --> 00:00:37.411
keep trickling out of the sand
at the foot of the beach.

00:00:37.761 --> 00:00:41.755
That's water that soaked in at high
tide, still finding its way out.

00:00:42.105 --> 00:00:46.841
This is the beach's soaked sand, with
every gap between the grains full of water.

00:00:47.191 --> 00:00:49.328
And water moves through those gaps.

00:00:49.678 --> 00:00:51.396
The question is how fast.

00:00:52.646 --> 00:00:58.126
Take a metre of each of four soils, and
put the same push of water on top of each.

00:00:58.476 --> 00:01:02.841
Gravel has big gaps, and water gets
through it in about thirty seconds.

00:01:03.191 --> 00:01:04.909
Sand takes about an hour.

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:08.835
Silt, which is finer still,
takes about a month and a half.

00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:12.065
Compacted clay takes about eleven years.

00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:17.384
These are typical values, and real
soils vary, but the spread is enormous.

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:22.192
Water passes through gravel about ten
million times as easily as through clay.

00:01:22.542 --> 00:01:26.768
How easily water passes through a
soil is called its permeability.

00:01:27.118 --> 00:01:30.478
So here's the obvious idea:
a dam must be watertight,

00:01:30.478 --> 00:01:32.876
so build it from the
tightest soil there is.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.245
But clay isn't watertight, only slow.

00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:40.775
To judge a dam, we need to see
what the water inside it is doing.

00:01:42.025 --> 00:01:45.740
Push a clear straw down into
the wet sand of the beach.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.248
Water climbs up the straw
and settles at a level.

00:01:49.598 --> 00:01:51.502
That level is called the head.

00:01:51.852 --> 00:01:55.521
Water always flows from
high head to low head.

00:01:55.871 --> 00:02:00.793
Up the beach, the water in the sand still
stands high, but the sea has dropped.

00:02:01.143 --> 00:02:05.787
So the water flows down through the sand,
and seeps out at the foot of the beach.

00:02:06.137 --> 00:02:10.642
Engineers sink thin tubes like this
into dams, and read the levels in them.

00:02:10.992 --> 00:02:13.267
They're called piezometers.

00:02:14.517 --> 00:02:16.236
Now look inside a dam.

00:02:16.586 --> 00:02:21.369
This one is twenty-four metres tall,
drawn to scale, standing on solid rock.

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:26.595
First, build it all from one ordinary
soil, the silt from the race.

00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:31.729
Fill the lake, and the water soaks in and
slowly finds its level inside the dam.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.376
The tube nearest the lake
reads almost the lake's level.

00:02:35.726 --> 00:02:40.277
The one in the middle reads lower,
and the one at the back lower still.

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:43.627
And the back slope weeps:
water seeps out of

00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:46.246
its face, up to ten
metres above the ground.

00:02:46.596 --> 00:02:50.590
That's the foot of the beach at
low tide, on the side of a dam.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:55.630
Through each metre along the dam, about
three buckets of water leak out every day.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.256
The obvious fix is to make it tighter.

00:02:59.506 --> 00:03:03.268
So build the same dam all of
clay, a hundred times tighter.

00:03:03.618 --> 00:03:07.101
The leak falls a hundredfold,
to about a cup a day.

00:03:07.451 --> 00:03:09.355
Now let the water soak in.

00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:13.977
A clay dam takes far longer to get
there, but it gets there in the end.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:18.228
Once it has, the water inside stands
exactly where it stood before.

00:03:20.578 --> 00:03:24.015
A hundred times tighter,
and not one bit drier.

00:03:24.365 --> 00:03:24.968
Why?

00:03:26.218 --> 00:03:30.278
In eighteen fifty-five, in a
hospital courtyard in Dijon, an

00:03:30.278 --> 00:03:34.067
engineer named Henry Darcy ran
water down a column of sand.

00:03:34.417 --> 00:03:37.597
The column stood taller than
a person, and he measured

00:03:37.597 --> 00:03:40.314
the water pressure at its
top and at its bottom.

00:03:40.664 --> 00:03:43.764
He had built the city's
water supply, and he wanted

00:03:43.764 --> 00:03:45.819
to size the sand filters that clean it.

00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:49.509
He found that the flow grows
with the drop in head, and

00:03:49.509 --> 00:03:52.206
shrinks with the distance
the water has to travel.

00:03:52.556 --> 00:03:55.316
Here's his test laid
on its side: a pipe of

00:03:55.316 --> 00:03:57.990
sand between two tanks,
with tubes along it.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.845
The levels in the tubes fall in a straight
line, from one tank to the other.

00:04:03.195 --> 00:04:07.281
Swap the sand for
gravel, or silt, or clay.

00:04:07.631 --> 00:04:11.532
The flow changes enormously, but
the line doesn't move at all.

00:04:11.882 --> 00:04:14.402
Through any one material,
the head falls in a

00:04:14.402 --> 00:04:17.037
straight line, however
tight the material is.

00:04:17.387 --> 00:04:20.220
That's why the all-clay dam was no drier.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.978
But what if the pipe
isn't all one material?

00:04:24.328 --> 00:04:29.250
Fill the middle fifth of the pipe with
clay, and leave sand on both sides.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.758
Now practically the whole
drop happens inside the plug.

00:04:33.108 --> 00:04:37.488
The tubes on the left read the same as
the left tank, and the tubes on the

00:04:37.488 --> 00:04:41.653
right read the same as the right tank,
to within a tenth of a millimetre.

00:04:42.003 --> 00:04:45.300
The same flow has to pass
through every part of the pipe.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.630
Sand lets it through
with almost no push, but

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:51.223
clay needs a steep drop
to force it through.

00:04:51.573 --> 00:04:53.802
So the clay takes nearly all of it.

00:04:56.652 --> 00:04:58.974
Now put the plug inside the dam.

00:04:59.324 --> 00:05:04.758
The dam becomes Darcy's pipe: sand,
a plug of clay, then sand again.

00:05:05.108 --> 00:05:08.328
A core of clay runs up
the middle, about a fifth

00:05:08.328 --> 00:05:11.331
of the dam's width, with
sand on both sides.

00:05:11.681 --> 00:05:15.721
Of the twenty metres of head, the
core takes all but half a metre.

00:05:16.071 --> 00:05:18.718
The back half of the dam stays dry.

00:05:19.068 --> 00:05:21.948
It does leak more than
the all-clay dam, a little

00:05:21.948 --> 00:05:24.037
over a litre a day through each metre.

00:05:24.387 --> 00:05:28.149
That's about four times as much,
and yet it's the better dam.

00:05:28.499 --> 00:05:29.149
Here's why.

00:05:29.499 --> 00:05:32.639
Soil is strong because its
grains press on each other,

00:05:32.639 --> 00:05:35.722
and water pressure in the
gaps takes that press away.

00:05:36.072 --> 00:05:39.212
At the foot of the one-soil
dam's back slope, the

00:05:39.212 --> 00:05:41.691
water carries about two
fifths of the weight.

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.601
Behind the clay core it
carries almost none,

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:47.707
so the grains there press hard, and grip.

00:05:48.057 --> 00:05:50.286
A dam doesn't have to be watertight.

00:05:50.636 --> 00:05:54.026
It has to keep the water pressure
out of the part that holds it up.

00:05:55.876 --> 00:05:58.105
Real earth dams are built this way.

00:05:58.455 --> 00:06:01.706
Here's a sixty-metre dam from
the course, drawn to scale.

00:06:02.056 --> 00:06:05.896
Read it from the middle out:
a tight core, then zones

00:06:05.896 --> 00:06:08.511
of coarser soil and rock
toward the outside.

00:06:08.861 --> 00:06:13.001
The core holds the water back, and
the coarse outer zones give the

00:06:13.001 --> 00:06:16.895
dam its weight and let any water
that gets past run out freely.

00:06:17.245 --> 00:06:20.365
The sand on each side of
the core has a job of its

00:06:20.365 --> 00:06:23.050
own, and we'll come to
it later in the series.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.533
Most of the world's big dams are
heaps of earth and rock like this.

00:06:28.683 --> 00:06:31.543
Shuangjiangkou in China, set
to become the tallest dam

00:06:31.543 --> 00:06:34.524
in the world, is built
around a core of tight soil.

00:06:35.774 --> 00:06:38.607
But every dam leaks, even this one.

00:06:38.957 --> 00:06:42.440
Ours lets a little over a litre
a day through every metre.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:46.227
Where does that water go, and
what does it do on the way?
